Z||000001||Leadfoot||07-02-2001||05:29 PM||||It depends on your driving characteristics.  Look at this site.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com/keepthat.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.americancustomeng.com/keepthat.htm</A> <P>It tells you things you should do to increase the life of your 2.8L.  I wish I read this article about a month ago cause it could have saved me a complete rebuild.   <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierodude:<BR><B>i talked to a guy at a fiero shop and he told me that 2.8 start pucking out exacly around 200km is this true?? if he's lying just to get me to buy a new motor im gonna kick his ass , but even so the thought of my motor dieing makes me shiver!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||07-02-2001||05:49 PM||||What genious decided to use that font on the link posted above?  I can barely read it.<P>Anyway, the original 2.8 on my car lasted 203k miles, it would have lasted longer if I wasn't dumb enough to think  my oil pressure sender was going bad and not that my oil pressure was really going downhill.  Also the oil passages were really clogged so it wasn't getting that much oil anyway.  I bet if the oil changes had been done religiously the engine could have made it to 250k miles easily.  So I would say maintenance and driving style are the 2 biggest factors in longevity of the 2.8.||||reg||3
Z||000003||mrfiero||07-02-2001||06:33 PM||||It really does depend on driving and maintenance habits.  I had a 2.8 give out at 105K miles and I have owned others with 150K miles.  It just depends on how you care for it.<P>Any engine over 100K miles should be serviced more frequently or at least watched over more closely.  I always check the oil level/condition when I fill the gas tank on my current GT with 105K miles, but not on my 16K mile GT.  It doesn't leak or burn any oil, so I don't pay as much attention to it (plus I drive it less than 500 miles a year).<P>I say if your car is running & driving fine as it is leave it alone.....unless you want to upgrade to a bigger motor.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||07-02-2001||06:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>What genious decided to use that font on the link posted above?  I can barely read it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah! No kidding.  At first I thought that my eye glass perscription ran out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> But seriously, what he described in the part labeled "third" was the exact same thing that happened to me.  I $h!t you not, my car gave out at exactly 20 miles + - a couple of blocks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||07-02-2001||06:49 PM||||I've cleared 250k miles on five. Just listen for the timing chain and change the oil.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 07-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||07-03-2001||11:58 AM||||Well, I never liked the 2.8, and I've had THREE go out on me with *less* than 100k miles. one with only 60k.  Not one has lasted me over 10kmiles of my ownership. (disclaimer: I don't know what was done, or not done, prior to owning the cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I personally think they are horrible engines, neither powerful or smooth when working, and usually not even that. But that's just my expierence.<P>I say replace it (or rather, start saving to do so). With darn near anything you can find, you will have a MORE reliable car, and a more powerful one too.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
